OE 10: Search Linear using list_issues with query "Finley" or "owner report" (or similar) to find the downstream report item. The agent finds OPS-100 "May Monthly Owner Report - Finley Properties", state state_OPS_2 (In Progress), project proj_002. Its description ties Robert Finley to the Mesa Vista portfolio. Airtable carries no owner field at all, so this link has to come from Linear or Slack: besides this description it is stated in Slack messages 831d2b6760205432a20487e2664a607e and a6779a055eaf5fb1893d0ed6d92e3b39 and 2687eb8d7cae501ea99b8c8305f12217, and in three OPS-100 comments. HubSpot is not a route to it, because it files Finley under company comp_mesaverde "Mesa Verde Investments" while the Mesa Vista deals are split across three other companies including comp_riogrande.

OE 11: Call list_comments with issueId "OPS-100" to read its four comments. comment_5a6d779a715f587392dd00b9c8dbbd4a (Brooke Phillips, 2026-05-26) repeats the 94 percent occupancy figure, and comment_42a514c0161254a7992a137d50d3be45 (2026-06-02) says "moving this to Done" while the issue still reads state_OPS_2. The same pattern of a record whose title or narrative outruns its state appears again on the pair OPS-39 "Q2 Make-Ready Budget Reconciliation" (state_OPS_3, In Review) and OPS-93 "Q2 Make-Ready Budget Reconciliation - Approved and Closed" (state_OPS_1, Todo, completed_at null), where the issue whose title claims closure is the one sitting in an earlier state and carrying the whole discussion. The 94 percent figure is traceable to Lisa herself: it appears in her own Slack message and in this comment repeating it back, and nowhere else in connection with Robert Finley or Mesa Vista. The same numeral does occur elsewhere in the universe on unrelated objects, including a HubSpot deal reporting Oakfield Commons occupancy, so the check is whether any record independently supports the figure for THIS portfolio, and none does.

OE 12: Call list_bases and list_tables_for_base with baseId "appPropertyOps" to enumerate the Airtable surface. The agent finds exactly two tables, tblMakeReady (120 records) and tblMaintenanceTickets (50 records). Call get_table_schema with baseId "appPropertyOps" and tables ["tblMakeReady", "tblMaintenanceTickets"] to confirm the make-ready fields fldUnit, fldTurnStatus, fldMoveOut, fldTargetReady and fldNotes2, and that fldTurnStatus offers three options: selSched (Scheduled), selProg (In Progress) and selReady (Ready).

OE 13: Establish which property is Harry Harris's before pulling any of his rows, because Airtable has no owner field. QuickBooks invoice 113714702211 (DocNumber 4422, CustomerRef Harry Harris) carries the CustomerMemo "Confirmation of lease renewal processing - Unit 14, Sunset Ridge Apartments, October 2026" and a PrivateNote naming the Sunset Ridge Unit 14 renewal fee, which is the cleanest bridge from the owner name to the property. Invoice 110274597983 (DocNumber 4418, $325.00) is a near-duplicate lease-renewal fee on the same unit billed to Simone Okafor, so the memo must be read on the Harris invoice specifically. Then search Airtable using search_records with baseId "appPropertyOps", table "tblMakeReady" and query "Sunset Ridge" to pull his cluster. The agent finds 7 rows across 3 unit strings and zero rows in selReady: rec987aae7d522057, recf50eb955a10651, rec2471fac3f9ae51 and reca06d89f1a4ac5b on Sunset Ridge 309C, rec98bdfeec73545e and rec7d202aed68c95c on Sunset Ridge 104B, and reca8230a8fd9ff51 on Sunset Ridge Unit 14. The Unit 14 row is a Tanya Mitchell delinquency row whose turn is conditional on the balance going unresolved rather than a routine turn, and the eviction framing around it comes from sibling records rather than from this row, and the bare string "Unit 14" collides across several properties, so it must be qualified by property whenever it is named.

OE 14: Search Airtable using search_records with baseId "appPropertyOps", table "tblMakeReady" and query "Mesa Vista" to pull Robert Finley's cluster. The agent finds 8 rows across 4 unit strings: rec23600780ef4053 and rec35a6c4f2e50657 on Mesa Vista 107A (both selProg), reca4aa17f0755b55, rec4081fd2ccde95a and rec591a0f70432651 on Mesa Vista 207A, rec88734a4fdfde57 on Mesa Vista 310C (selSched), and recbd087a4abd605b and recc8534b3fd13954 on Mesa Vista 4C. Four Mesa Vista units carry make-ready rows, which contradicts Lisa's "one unit still in make-ready" claim from OE 8.

OE 15: Read rec88734a4fdfde57 (Mesa Vista 310C, selSched, fldMoveOut 2026-05-22, fldTargetReady 2026-05-22). Its fldNotes2 reads "Move-out inspection booked for July 16. Maintenance flagged possible subfloor issue under bathroom tile - needs assessment before scope is finalized." This is the only item in either cluster that is both unresolved and untracked: the turn scope cannot be set until someone assesses the subfloor, and the assessment exists nowhere except this row's fldNotes2. It is distinct from the other open work the agent will find, because that work is already carried somewhere. The roof evaluation in OE 18 has its own ticket MT-2026-047, and the Ridgeview follow-up has a booked walk-through on 2026-07-13, so neither of those can quietly disappear at hand-off. Mesa Vista 310C is a single-row unit string, so it is uniquely addressable.

