OE 28: Call list_events with fullText "Portfolio Review" across the other participants' calendars (aurora.winona@starpm.com, brooke.phillips@starpm.com, patricia.nguyen@starpm.com, teresa.wood@starpm.com) and confirm each hit with get_event. That query returns every mid-year portfolio review in the universe, including "Linda Castillo Mid-Year Portfolio Review" (base id epax0kiwoq0ygmqxezm2pax18l, 2026-05-26, Lisa holds a row and accepted). Castillo is Patricia's owner under the OE 3 split, so she is out of scope here and no conclusion in this task may rest on her. David Shea returns nothing at all, because he has no calendar presence anywhere. Within Lisa's two owners the agent finds two live events for the same owner one day apart, both status confirmed and neither cancelled: "Harry Harris Mid-Year Portfolio Review" (base id 1pon50ds1aevem63td6f7emdn3, 2026-06-02 12:15 to 12:45, five calendar rows including Lisa's row 1pon50ds1aevem63td6f7emdn3-b0504ab4, all four attendees accepted) and "Harry Harris Mid-Year Portfolio Review (Rescheduled)" (base id qqbwq3s2h7wh5udoek2940mffk, 2026-06-03 15:00 to 16:30, four calendar rows, Aurora Winona and Patricia Nguyen both declined, Teresa Wood accepted). Lisa holds no row on the rescheduled instance and is not an attendee of it, so it is reachable only by listing calendars she is not on.

OE 29: Call get_event on the Finley review to check the second owner's meeting. The agent finds "Robert Finley Mid-Year Portfolio Review", base id 8mwlxrq5w5oodwdpmvo83e00f2, on 2026-05-19 from 11:45 to 13:15, four rows including Lisa's row 8mwlxrq5w5oodwdpmvo83e00f2-b0504ab4, with Lisa Smith and Aurora Winona both declined and Robert Finley not an attendee at all. This contradicts comment_79dc83838bd65d678c48b5911f942412 from OE 4 on three counts: the meeting is in May rather than the first week of June, it runs 90 minutes rather than 60, and it sits in late morning rather than the afternoon. The Harris original carries the same defect against its own announcement, because Slack described it as a casual 45-minute morning call in late June while the event is 30 minutes at midday on 2026-06-02. A sweep of 2026-06-01 through 2026-06-09 returns no Finley event, so the meeting that comment describes does not exist. A separate event, "May Owner Report Review - Finley Properties" (base id ti5zt1xubdggbehtp79um9mim6, 2026-05-28 11:45 to 12:15, three rows, Lisa declined, Robert Finley accepted), is also a Finley review meeting that also looks unsettled, and it must not be mistaken for the mid-year review: it is the monthly May report review, a different deliverable. Neither owner's mid-year review ended up properly settled.

OE 30: Update the make-ready records that do not match the ground, using update_records_for_table with baseId "appPropertyOps", tableId "tblMakeReady" and a records array. Three rows across the two owners qualify, and they are exactly the selSched rows whose own evidence shows the work has already started. The agent moves rec98bdfeec73545e (Sunset Ridge 104B) to selProg because the sibling row records the repaint started on July 15, rec987aae7d522057 (Sunset Ridge 309C) to selProg because the crew question it was waiting on is answered and the vendor schedule is locked in, and rec8b679d92f30753 (Ridgeview) to selProg because the repair event has already happened and the work is invoiced. Mesa Vista 207A and Mesa Vista 4C each carry a selProg row alongside a selReady row on the same unit, which looks like the same defect shape, but both units were excluded upstream as ambiguous targets and neither is a correction target here. The other three selSched rows in these clusters must also be left alone: rec88734a4fdfde57 (Mesa Vista 310C) is waiting on a July 16 inspection that has not happened, reca06d89f1a4ac5b (Sunset Ridge 309C) is waiting on a utility transfer that no record resolves, and reca8230a8fd9ff51 (Sunset Ridge Unit 14) is conditional on an eviction that has not completed. S3 must decompose this into one criterion per content element (rec98bdfeec73545e set to selProg, rec987aae7d522057 set to selProg, rec8b679d92f30753 set to selProg) rather than one criterion covering the set.

OE 31: Resolve both owners' review meetings on the calendar. Both are required, because the prompt's condition fires on each of them independently. For Harris, the rescheduled instance is the one to act on, and because Lisa holds no row on it she cannot respond to it. She addresses it with delete_event or update_event using the eventId that list_events returned for it, which is a per-calendar row such as qqbwq3s2h7wh5udoek2940mffk-b6a1e41c on teresa.wood@starpm.com or qqbwq3s2h7wh5udoek2940mffk-0f82233a on brooke.phillips@starpm.com, not the bare base id. Either verb is correct provided the rescheduled instance is cancelled or retitled on the calendar the agent addresses, so that it no longer stands as a second live Harris review alongside the 2026-06-02 original. For Finley, the review sat on 2026-05-19 and is six weeks in the past, so responding to it settles nothing. The agent addresses row 8mwlxrq5w5oodwdpmvo83e00f2-b0504ab4 with update_event to move it to a workable date, or uses create_event to put a replacement review on the calendar. S3 must decompose this into one criterion per content element (the duplicated Harris review no longer stands as two live meetings, the Finley review is rescheduled or replaced).

OE 32: Look up the hand-off recipient using contacts_search_contacts with query "Brooke Phillips". The agent resolves contact_id c46d47256fd95ca6aca770c8dddda5eb, email brooke.phillips@starpm.com, job "Apartment Property Supervisor". There is exactly one Brooke Phillips in contacts, in Slack and in HubSpot, so the recipient is unambiguous.

OE 33: Create the hand-off email using create_draft with to ["brooke.phillips@starpm.com"], a subject naming the mid-year review hand-off for Harry Harris and Robert Finley, and a body carrying the specifics for both owners. Gmail in this universe is draft-only and there is no send tool, so the deliverable is the draft itself. The body must separate the two owners: Harris is operationally blocked, with every make-ready row on his Sunset Ridge units sitting in Scheduled or In Progress and none in a Ready state, against $0.00 of open receivable, plus two open delinquency records on Unit 14; Finley is cash-blocked, with $10,980.00 past due across invoices 2026-494, 2026-303 and 4421 plus $3,655.00 of credit memos that are unapplied and therefore do not reduce the balance, and an open roof ticket MT-2026-047. The body must also correct the spring read: the 94 percent occupancy and 97 percent collections figures have no supporting record, Mesa Vista has four units in make-ready rather than one, the closed water heater ticket belongs to a different portfolio, and the cleared late payment is a Sunset Ridge tenant rather than a Mesa Vista one. S3 must decompose this into one criterion per content element (recipient, Harris make-ready position, Harris receivable position, Finley receivable total, Finley unapplied credits, Finley open maintenance, the occupancy and collections correction, the make-ready count correction, the water heater correction, the late-payment correction).

OE 34: Post the progress update on the parent review item using save_comment with issueId "OPS-10" and a body describing where Lisa's half has landed. The comment covers both owners, the corrections made to the make-ready records, the calendar resolution, and the fact that the hand-off has gone to Brooke. The agent may also move OPS-10 out of state_OPS_0 using save_issue, since the issue's own comment thread has claimed two transitions that never took effect. That state change is optional and must not be graded: OPS-10 is the four-owner parent and Patricia's half is not in scope here, so an agent that leaves the state alone is equally correct.

OE 35: Open one new issue for the work that survives the hand-off using save_issue with title naming the unresolved item, team "team_001", project "proj_002" and a description. The item is the Mesa Vista 310C subfloor assessment from OE 15. It is the correct target because the prompt asks for an item so the work does not quietly disappear at hand-off, and the subfloor assessment is the only open item in either cluster that is carried nowhere: MT-2026-047 already exists as a ticket and the Ridgeview follow-up already has a booked walk-through, so neither of those needs a new item to survive. Exactly one issue is expected, matching the prompt's "a separate item". The created issue's own identifier cannot be predicted because team_001 has next_issue_number 1000, so it must be graded on title and description rather than on a number.

OE 36: Post the short version to the team using slack_send_message with channel_id "C006" and message summarising where Lisa's two owners sit. Brooke's own 2026-05-28 posts in that channel establish the precedent for owner review status landing there as a top-level message. The post is a condensed version of the email: the two owners named, the Harris turn position, the Finley past-due position, the one item still open, and the fact that the hand-off has gone to Brooke. S3 must decompose this into one criterion per content element (post lands in C006, both owners named, Finley past-due position stated, the open item named).
